            <ArticleTitle>Na6Si3S8O: the first example of a sulfide silicate exhibiting unusual tri-polymerized [Si3S8O]6- units without S-O bonds.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>13356-13359</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1039/c7dt03125d</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Generally, sulfur is inclined to combine with oxygen to form sulfate ions in a solid-state reaction. Interestingly, an extraordinary sulfide silicate, Na6Si3S8O, was successfully obtained in a vacuum-sealed silica tube for the first time. Its crystal structure features novel 1D straight chains formed by the interconnection of tri-polymerized [Si3S8O]6- and [Na4S5O]11- units, demonstrating the first sulfide silicate exhibiting S-Na-O and S-Si-O connections without S-O bonds. Moreover, Na6Si3S8O is also verified as a promising birefringent material owing to its large birefringence (Δn = 0.215-0.094 from 0.3 to 4.0 μm).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
